# Runtime machine translation (see src/i18n/translationEngine.js) is ON by default, with no
# setup: strings missing from a language's pack are translated in the visitor's browser by
# Google's free keyless endpoint (src/i18n/liveTranslator.js) and cached there.
# Copy to .env (or .env.production) only to change that:
#   VITE_TRANSLATE_URL=off      no runtime translation (no request leaves the browser)
#   VITE_TRANSLATE_URL=<url>    the museum's own service instead, as below.
#
# A LibreTranslate-compatible endpoint: POST { q: [...], source, target, format } and
# answers { translatedText: [...] }. Self-host LibreTranslate, or put a small proxy of the
# museum's own in front of Google/DeepL that adds the real key on the server.
# It must send Access-Control-Allow-Origin for the museum's address.
# VITE_TRANSLATE_URL=https://translate.example.org/translate
#
# Optional, and PUBLIC: every VITE_ variable is compiled into the website's JavaScript, where
# anyone can read it. Only a key meant to be public (rate-limited, for this site) goes here —
# never a Google Cloud, DeepL or other paid key.
# VITE_TRANSLATE_KEY=
#
# For scripts/generateTranslations.mjs (run on your own PC; never compiled into the site).
# The script reads these from the shell's environment, not from this file:
# GOOGLE_TRANSLATE_KEY=
# LIBRETRANSLATE_URL=https://translate.example.org/translate
# LIBRETRANSLATE_KEY=
